  "query_preview": "..\nAnother round of heavy rainfall is possible tonight for Guam and\nRota, so the previously-issued headline continues through tonight. \nSurface convergence continues to be concentrated south of the \nMarianas, so the zones remain split as drier air from the north will \nmove over Saipan and Tinian Thursday morning before spreading to Guam\nand Rota Thursday afternoon. Isolated thunderstorms remain in the \nforecast for quite some time as upper-level divergence is favorable \nover the entire region. Gentle to moderate west and northwest winds \nwill decrease to light and variable on Thursday across the region. As\nfor the weekend, showers will be on and off, with Sunday and Monday \nlooking to be the driest for Guam and Rota. On the other hand, a\ncol may set up over Saipan and Tinian, potentially bringing \nrelatively dry conditions through the entire weekend. There is a \nlarge disagreement between the models regarding a potential tropical\ndisturbance developing west of the region, so showers top out at 30 \npercent for the region due to the high uncertainty. Next week,\nscattered showers may return for all islands.",
